  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale:\n   - We address the known challenge of step-function design for maximizing minimum separation between a function and its shifted version\n   - The core insight is that instead of optimizing the entire function at once, we can focus on the boundaries of the support where most of the overlap occurs\n   - This approach leverages the observation that the highest overlap values typically occur either at the boundaries of the domain or when two function decreases overlap with their shifted counterparts\n   - By using a gradient-based method and pair-wise adjustments, we can efficiently explore the space of function configurations while maintaining the integral constraint\n   - The algorithm constructs candidate functions by systematically adjusting pairs of points in directions identified by gradient information, ensuring both feasibility and domain boundaries\n   - This differs fundamentally from the previous algorithm's stochastic perturbation approach by being gradient-directed and focusing on boundary-related adjustments\n\n2. Mathematical specification:\n   - Formulation:\n     - Optimization variables: $h \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$ with $0 \\leq h_i \\leq 1 \\forall i$\n     - Constraint: $\\sum_{i=1}^n h_i = \\frac{n}{2}$\n     - Objective: $\\min C_5 = \\max_{k=-n+1,...,n-1} \\int_0^2 h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx$\n   - Discretization:\n     - $n=400$ grid points with $dx=2.0/n$\n     - Convolution computed with $\\text{np.correlate}(h, 1-h, \\text{mode}=\\text{'full'})$\n   - Change matrix interpretation:\n     - Elements $C_{ij} = h_i - h_j$ for $|i-j| \\leq n-1$\n     - Each entry contributes to specific shifts with predefined $+1$ or $-1$ coefficients\n\n3. Implementation procedure:\n   a. Validation and initialization:\n    - Input $h_{init}$ (400 samples)\n    - Project feasible to satisfy $\\sum h_i = 200$ (with $dx=2.0/400$)\n    - Precompute the change matrix accounting for shift directions\n  b. Core update function:\n    - Identify top-gradient indices using precomputed change matrix\n    - Select high-gradient and low-gradient subsets ($N_{high}=40, N_{low}=40$)\n    - Evaluate collaboration bonus between high and low pairs \n    - Compute directional derivative for various $d \\in [-0.01, 0.01]$\n    - Execute coordinate descent: update $h_i,d(h_j)$ for top candidates\n    c. Constraint handling:\n     - After each update, introduce small perturbations $ \\epsilon \\sim \\mathcal{U}[-dx, dx] $ to any 10% of points\n     - Project to feasible region (enforce sum=200, clip to $[0,1]$)\n     - Overflow redistribution: recalculate projected changes\n  d. Termination:\n    - Evaluate $C_5$ for candidate solution\n    - Compare against best-known record (0.38085)\n\n4. Search choices and variations:\n   - Initial parameters:\n    1. $N_{active}=40$ for gradient magnitude estimation\n    2. $N_{candidates}=80$ for candidate selection ($40$ high, $40$ low)\n    3. Directional search range: $d \\in [-0.03, 0.03]$\n    4. Convergence threshold: $\\triangle C_5 < 10^{-6}$ for 100 consecutive trials\n   - Parameter schedule:\n    - Initial time window: 200 seconds\n    - Subsequent expansion: add 200 seconds intervals\n    - Strategy bump-up: every 10 iterations with $15\\%$ parameter adjustment\n    - Progressive refinement: gradually increase $N_{candidates}$ from 120 to 300\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness:\n   - Dominant operations:\n    - Gradient calculation: $\\mathcal{O}(n^2)$ per update\n    - Candidate evaluation: $\\mathcal{O}(n)$ (gradient-established $+$ matrix projection)\n   - Time estimates per iteration: 2-5 seconds\n   - Batch processing: paralleled operations scheduled automatically\n   - Overhead management: monitoring of change matrix updates, directional perturbations, and feasibility projections\n   - Early termination: if best $C_5$ would exceed 0.38085\n   - Fallback method: if optimization stalls, switch to stochastic coordinate perturbation\n\n6. Validation and return contract:\n   - Pre-checks:\n    1. $0 \\leq h_i \\leq 1 \\forall i$\n    2. $\\left|\\sum h_i - 200\\right| < 1e-4$\n    3. $C_5$ computed via $\\text{compute\\_c5}(h) = \\max(\\text{np.correlate}(h, 1-h, \\text{mode}=\\text{'full'}) \\times (2.0/400)$\n   - Candidate acceptance:\n    - Requires $C_5 < \\text{initial }C_5_5$\n    - Records accepted candidate values\n   - Terminal output:\n    - Tuple: ($h\\_values$, $C\\_5\\_bound$, $n\\_points=400$)\n    - Final score computation from recomputed correlation\n    - Fresh projection if constraint violation within 0.01%",
